<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<topic id="Authentication-NTLM-Kerberos" revisionNumber="1">
  <developerConceptualDocument
    xmlns="http://ddue.schemas.microsoft.com/authoring/2003/5"
    xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">

    <introduction>
      <para>
        Microsoft Exchange (on-premises) and other servers in Windows domain environments often support Windows
        authentication using the <literal>NTLM</literal> and/or <literal>GSSAPI</literal> (Kerberos) SASL
        mechanisms. These allow an application to authenticate using domain credentials, or as the currently
        logged-in user without needing to know the user's password at all.
      </para>
      <autoOutline />
    </introduction>

    <section address="Ntlm">
      <title>NTLM</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          MailKit includes two implementations of the <literal>NTLM</literal> mechanism:
        </para>
        <table>
          <tableHeader>
            <row>
              <entry><para>Class</para></entry>
              <entry><para>Description</para></entry>
            </row>
          </tableHeader>
          <row>
            <entry><para><codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanismNtlm</codeEntityReference></para></entry>
            <entry>
              <para>
                A fully managed implementation that is available on all target frameworks and platforms. It
                requires an explicit user name and password.
              </para>
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry><para><codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanismNtlmNative</codeEntityReference></para></entry>
            <entry>
              <para>
                Uses the operating system's implementation via
                <codeEntityReference>T:System.Net.Security.NegotiateAuthentication</codeEntityReference>. Only
                available on .NET 8 and later, and only on platforms where NTLM is supported (Windows, or
                Linux/macOS with the appropriate GSSAPI libraries installed). It can authenticate as the
                currently logged-in user.
              </para>
            </entry>
          </row>
        </table>
        <para>
          When MailKit selects <literal>NTLM</literal> automatically (see <link xlink:href="Authentication"/>),
          it uses <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanismNtlmNative</codeEntityReference> if it is
          supported on the current platform and falls back to
          <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanismNtlm</codeEntityReference> otherwise.
        </para>
        <para>
          To use the managed implementation explicitly, pass the domain as part of the
          <codeEntityReference>T:System.Net.NetworkCredential</codeEntityReference>. Setting the
          <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanismNtlm.ServicePrincipalName</codeEntityReference>
          and enabling <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanismNtlm.AllowChannelBinding</codeEntityReference>
          help protect against relay and man-in-the-middle attacks:
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\AuthenticationExamples.cs" region="AuthenticateWithNtlm"/>
        <para>
          To authenticate as the currently logged-in user with the native implementation, use the default
          constructor, which uses <codeEntityReference>P:System.Net.CredentialCache.DefaultNetworkCredentials</codeEntityReference>:
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\AuthenticationExamples.cs" region="AuthenticateWithNtlmNative"/>
        <alert class="warning">
          <para>
            NTLM is an old protocol with known weaknesses: it is vulnerable to relay attacks, and the cryptography
            it uses (HMAC-MD5 for NTLMv2) is weak by modern standards. Only use NTLM over a TLS connection, and
            prefer Kerberos (<literal>GSSAPI</literal>) or OAuth 2.0 (see <link xlink:href="Authentication-OAuth2"/>)
            whenever the server supports them. Microsoft has deprecated NTLM in favor of Kerberos.
          </para>
        </alert>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="Gssapi">
      <title>Kerberos (GSSAPI)</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanismGssapi</codeEntityReference> implements the
          <literal>GSSAPI</literal> mechanism using Kerberos via
          <codeEntityReference>T:System.Net.Security.NegotiateAuthentication</codeEntityReference>. It is only
          available on .NET 8 and later; use
          <codeEntityReference>M:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanism.IsSupported(System.String)</codeEntityReference>
          to check whether the current platform supports it. On Linux and macOS, the system's GSSAPI libraries
          must be installed and the user must have a valid Kerberos ticket (for example, obtained using
          <literal>kinit</literal>).
        </para>
        <para>
          Kerberos authenticates to a specific service, identified by a <legacyItalic>service principal name</legacyItalic>
          (SPN). If <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanismNegotiateBase.ServicePrincipalName</codeEntityReference>
          is not set, MailKit uses <literal>SMTPSVC/&lt;host&gt;</literal>, which is only correct for SMTP. For IMAP
          and POP3, set it explicitly (usually <literal>imap/&lt;host&gt;</literal> or
          <literal>pop/&lt;host&gt;</literal>, but this depends on how the server is configured):
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\AuthenticationExamples.cs" region="AuthenticateWithGssapi"/>
        <alert class="note">
          <para>
            The host name used in the SPN must be the server's fully qualified host name as known to the Kerberos
            realm. Connecting via an IP address or an alias (CNAME) commonly causes Kerberos authentication to fail.
          </para>
        </alert>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="ChannelBinding">
      <title>Channel binding</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          For both <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanismNtlmNative</codeEntityReference> and
          <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanismGssapi</codeEntityReference>, set
          <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanismNegotiateBase.DesiredChannelBinding</codeEntityReference>
          (for example, to <codeInline>ChannelBindingKind.Endpoint</codeInline>) to bind the authentication to the TLS
          session. After authenticating,
          <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanism.NegotiatedChannelBinding</codeEntityReference>
          indicates whether channel binding was used. Servers that enforce Extended Protection for Authentication
          (EPA) require channel binding.
        </para>
      </content>
    </section>

    <relatedTopics>
      <link xlink:href="Authentication"/>
      <link xlink:href="Authentication-OAuth2"/>
      <link xlink:href="Connecting-To-A-Server"/>
    </relatedTopics>
  </developerConceptualDocument>
</topic>
